The second probe lacks any phenolic OH, eliminating B (and any phenoxyl pathway) as a possibility for the residual light-dependent signal. D is chemically inaccessible from either probe structure. E is added post-irradiation and is identical across all lanes/conditions. This narrows the choice to A vs. C.

C would be an intrinsic, light-independent fragmentation product of the bicyclo[4.2.0] core and therefore cannot explain why the gel still shows a clear light-vs-dark difference that requires both 417 nm illumination and the added thioxanthone. The only species that satisfies all constraints (shared between probes, strictly light- and sensitizer-dependent, and able to drive low-level protein labeling via known side reactions such as 1O2 or direct H-abstraction) is therefore the thioxanthone itself.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The pattern is clear: efficient labeling requires the phenolic OH for rapid one-electron oxidation by the excited thioxanthenone, yielding the phenoxyl radical that drives the bulk of the covalent attachment. Once that site is replaced by a benzylic alcohol the dominant pathway disappears, leaving only the much weaker residual signal. That residual increment is still strictly light-dependent and absent from the dark control, which points directly to continued (but less efficient) action of the same sensitizer—either through singlet-oxygen generation or direct H-abstraction at the benzylic position or protein side chains.  

Colleagues favoring C assume a retro-[2+2] fragmentation of the bicyclo[4.2.0] core occurs readily under 417 nm sensitization, yet the second probe still shows a markedly smaller contrast than the first; if fragmentation were the shared, phenol-independent route one would expect comparable labeling efficiency in both cases, not the observed sharp drop. The click reagent is added after irradiation, so it cannot explain any light-specific difference, and carbene formation is structurally inaccessible.  

A hybrid view therefore fits the data best: the thioxanthenone remains the essential actor for the residual labeling once the phenoxyl-radical channel is removed. This keeps the explanation consistent with the shared experimental variables (sensitizer, wavelength, lysate concentration) without invoking an unproven fragmentation pathway whose yield would have to be both low and curiously insensitive to the structural change that so dramatically attenuates the signal.}

